    "candidate_text": "For each \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), \\(x^n\\to0\\), whereas \\(f_n(1)=1\\) for every \\(n\\). Hence \\((f_n)\\) converges pointwise to\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIf \\((f_n)\\) converged uniformly, its uniform limit would necessarily equal this pointwise limit. Fix \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\). For every \\(N\\in\\mathbb Z_+\\), take \\(n=N\\) and \\(x=2^{-1/N}\\). Then \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), so \\(f(x)=0\\), and\n\\[\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus, for every \\(N\\), there exist \\(n\\ge N\\) and \\(x\\in[0,1]\\) such that \\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\ge\\varepsilon\\), which is precisely the negation of uniform convergence to \\(f\\). Therefore \\((f_n)\\) does not converge uniformly on \\([0,1]\\).",
